    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The traditional religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> worships Olodumare, the supreme god, and the orisha, divine powers such as Obatala, Ogun, Shango, Oshun and Yemoja, honoured with prayer, sacrifice, music, dance and possession.</li><li>At its heart lies Ifa, a system of divination whose priests memorise a vast oral literature of verses; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> proclaimed it a masterpiece of the oral heritage of humanity in 2005.</li><li>Carried across the Atlantic by enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, it gave rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and related traditions, which made the orisha some of the most widely worshipped African gods in the world.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The sacred grove of the river goddess Oshun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, one of the last stretches of primary high forest in southern Nigeria, was restored with the help of the Austrian artist Susanne Wenger, who became a priestess of the orisha, and is now a World Heritage Site.</p></aside>
    </section>

