    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oduduwa.jpg" alt="Oduduwa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oduduwa_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tunde Akangbe, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> are one of the largest peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, today more than forty million strong, living mainly in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and in neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>. Their religion, sometimes called Isese, “tradition”, grew over many centuries among their towns and kingdoms and has no founder or fixed date of origin.</p><p>Its sacred centre is Ile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>, which Yoruba tradition regards as the place where the world was created. In one widely told myth, the supreme god Olodumare sent the orisha Obatala down from heaven with a shell of earth and a hen to make dry land on the primeval waters; when Obatala fell asleep drunk on palm wine, his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> completed the task, founded Ife and became its first king. The sons of Oduduwa, according to tradition, founded the other Yoruba kingdoms, and their rulers still trace their authority to him.</p><p>Archaeology confirms Ife’s antiquity. The city flourished from around 1000 CE, and between about the 12th and 15th centuries its artists created terracotta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-alloy heads of extraordinary naturalism, probably representing rulers and used in royal ritual. Their discovery in the 20th century astonished European scholars, one of whom, the German ethnologist Leo Frobenius, refused to believe they could be African and imagined them as relics of Atlantis.</p><p>Each Yoruba town and lineage honoured its own orisha, and the religion was always plural and local, with different gods prominent in different places. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>, which from the 17th century dominated much of the Yoruba country and the lands to the west, spread the worship of Shango, the orisha of thunder, remembered in tradition as an early king of Oyo. The neighbouring Fon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> adopted Ifa divination as Fa, and Yoruba gods entered the religion of their vodun.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Above all things stands Olodumare, also called Olorun, “the owner of heaven”, the supreme creator, who is rarely worshipped directly and is approached through the orisha, the divine powers who carry out his will in the world. Tradition speaks of four hundred and one orisha, and their number is open: some are forces of nature, others deified ancestors, kings and heroes.</p><p>Among the most widely worshipped are Obatala, the orisha of purity, wisdom and the white cloth, who shapes human bodies; Orunmila, the witness of destiny and the source of Ifa; Eshu, the messenger and trickster, guardian of crossroads and doorways, who carries sacrifices to the gods and enforces order, and whom Christian missionaries wrongly identified with the devil; Ogun, the orisha of iron, war and hunting, patron of smiths and, today, of drivers and mechanics; Shango, the orisha of thunder, lightning and justice, armed with a double-headed axe; Oshun, the goddess of the river at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a>, of sweet water, love and fertility; Yemoja, mother of many orisha and goddess of the waters; and Oya, goddess of wind, storms and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> river.</p><p>Every person has an ori, literally “head”: an inner self and personal destiny chosen before birth in heaven, which must be honoured and fulfilled. All beings and words possess ashe, power or vital force, which the orisha, kings and priests hold in abundance. The ideal of human life is iwa pele, gentle and good character, together with wealth, children and long life.</p><p>The ancestors remain part of the family and community; they are honoured with offerings and appear among the living in the masquerades of Egungun, whose dancers embody them. Ancestors may be reborn within their families, a belief reflected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> names such as Babatunde, “father has returned”, and Yetunde, “mother has returned”.</p><p>Worship centres on offerings and sacrifice, ebo: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a>, food, drink and animals offered at shrines to win the favour of the orisha and to restore balance in a person’s life. In ceremonies of drumming, song and dance, the orisha may “mount” their initiated devotees in possession trance, speaking and acting through them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion has no written scripture; its sacred knowledge is oral, and its greatest body of it is Ifa. Ifa is both an orisha, identified with the sage Orunmila, and a system of divination practised by priests called babalawo, “fathers of secrets”.</p><p>To consult Ifa, the babalawo casts sixteen sacred palm nuts or a divining chain of eight half seed pods, and marks the results in powder on a carved wooden tray whose border bears the face of Eshu. The marks form one of 256 signs, called odu, combinations of sixteen principal figures. Each odu is linked to a large number of verses, ese, which tell how gods, people and animals faced a problem in the past, what sacrifice they made and what followed. The babalawo recites the verses that fit the client’s situation, and the client recognises the one that applies to them; the reading ends with a prescribed sacrifice.</p><p>Training as a babalawo takes many years, and a master may know thousands of verses by heart. The corpus is a vast library of Yoruba myth, history, medicine, ethics and poetry, and in 2005 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> proclaimed the Ifa divination system a masterpiece of the oral and intangible heritage of humanity. Scholars such as William Bascom and the Yoruba scholar and babalawo Wande Abimbola have recorded and translated many of its verses since the mid-20th century.</p><p>Alongside Ifa, Yoruba religion is carried by oriki, praise poems that celebrate the deeds and character of each orisha, ancestor and lineage; by songs, chants and proverbs; and by the talking drums, which can reproduce the tones of the Yoruba language and speak praises and prayers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion has spread in two great movements: within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, through the influence of the Yoruba kingdoms, and across the Atlantic, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p><p>In the late 18th and 19th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> declined and collapsed, its capital abandoned in the 1830s, and long wars among the Yoruba states followed. Hundreds of thousands of captives were sold into the Atlantic slave trade, many of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, where Yoruba-speaking people were called Lucumí and Nagô. They rebuilt their religion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and freedom, often under the cover of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, identifying each orisha with a Catholic saint: Shango with Saint Barbara, Oshun with Our Lady of Charity, the patron of Cuba, and Babaluaye, the orisha of disease, with Saint Lazarus.</p><p>In Cuba the result was the religion called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a> or Regla de Ocha, with its own priests, initiations and Ifa diviners; in Brazil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a>, whose first great houses were founded by African priestesses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> in the early 19th century; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, the Orisha religion. After the Cuban revolution of 1959, emigrants carried Santería to Miami, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and beyond, and in 1993 the Supreme Court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> upheld the right of a Santería church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to practise animal sacrifice.</p><p>In West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, meanwhile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread rapidly among the Yoruba from the 19th century: missionaries arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a> in the 1840s, and the Yoruba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-ajayi-crowther">Samuel Ajayi Crowther</a>, a freed slave, became the first African Anglican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1864, while Islam spread from the north. Today most Yoruba are Christians or Muslims, but traditional festivals, kingship rituals and the consultation of Ifa remain part of public and private life, often alongside the newer faiths.</p><p>Since the 20th century the orisha have drawn new followers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including movements seeking to restore African forms of worship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ife"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ife.jpg" alt="Ife" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ile_Ife_aerial_scape.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oramfe, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion is organised around the cults of individual orisha, each with its own priests and priestesses, initiates, shrines, colours, drums, songs, taboos and festivals. Women hold high office in many cults, and the oldest houses of Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> are traditionally led by women, the mothers of the saints.</p><p>The babalawo, priests of Ifa, are consulted at every important moment of life: at the birth of a child, before a marriage, a journey or a business venture, and in illness and misfortune. Kings, oba, are sacred figures, and the Ooni of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> and the rulers of other towns preside over great annual festivals. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogboni-society">Ogboni society</a>, an association of elders devoted to the earth, held judicial and political power in many towns.</p><p>Festivals bring whole towns together. Every August the Osun-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a> festival draws thousands of worshippers and visitors to the river goddess’s sacred grove. Egungun masquerades bring the ancestors back among the living, and the Gelede masquerades of the western Yoruba honour the “mothers”, the powerful elder women of the community.</p><p>Twins are especially honoured among the Yoruba, who have one of the highest rates of twin births in the world; when a twin dies, a small wooden figure, ere ibeji, is carved and cared for in its place.</p><p>Followers of the orisha have often suffered hostility. Colonial and missionary authorities condemned their worship as idolatry; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> Candomblé ceremonies were subject to police control in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> until 1976; and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> today traditional worshippers are a minority who face pressure from both Christian and Muslim neighbours.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion is one of the most influential African religious traditions in history. Through the Atlantic diaspora, its gods are worshipped today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and beyond, and orisha worship is one of the clearest examples of an African civilisation surviving and recreating itself in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><p>Its art has a place among the world’s masterpieces: the heads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>, the carved doors and veranda posts of sculptors such as Olowe of Ise, beaded crowns, twin figures and Gelede masks. In the 1960s the artists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a> school, working with Susanne Wenger and others, renewed Yoruba art on the themes of the orisha.</p><p>Yoruba sacred music shaped the music of the Americas: the bata drums of Shango and the rhythms of orisha worship flowed into Cuban rumba and Afro-Cuban jazz, and the rhythms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> into Brazilian music. In literature, the Nobel laureate Wole Soyinka has made Ogun a central figure of his plays and essays.</p><p>Ifa, with its 256 signs and its immense oral corpus, has been studied as a system of knowledge and recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, and the Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove, protected as a World Heritage Site since 2005, is a reminder of the religion’s bond with the natural world. Its spread among people of every background in the Americas has made the Yoruba orisha global gods.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The traditional religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, centred on the sacred city of Ile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>, worships the supreme god Olodumare and the orisha through sacrifice, music, dance, possession and the Ifa divination, with its vast oral literature. Carried across the Atlantic by enslaved Yoruba, it lives on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> and other traditions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, as well as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>.</em></p>

